{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon EBS now provides visibility into EBS volume initialization status - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS) has introduced a new feature that allows users to monitor the status of EBS volume initialization when creating volumes from snapshots. This feature provides visibility into the downloading and writing process of storage blocks from Amazon S3 to the volume, ensuring fully provisioned performance once completed. The initialization rate can be unpredictable, leading to increased I/O latency and reduced performance during this period. With the new status feature, users can time their application launches to align with volume initialization completion, ensuring optimal performance. This status is available for all EBS volumes in all AWS Regions, including AWS GovCloud (US) and AWS China Regions. Users can access this feature through the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface (CLI), or AWS SDKs. When creating volumes with Provisioned IOPS, users can also view the estimated completion time for volume initialization. The volume initialization status is accessible by default for all EBS volumes","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/07/amazon-ebs-visibility-volume-initialization-status/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"New Amazon CloudWatch metrics to monitor EC2 instances exceeding I/O performance - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudWatch has introduced two new metrics to help monitor EC2 instances with EBS volumes that exceed I/O performance limits. These metrics, named \"Instance EBS IOPS Exceeded Check\" and \"Instance EBS Throughput Exceeded Check,\" measure if the driven IOPS or throughput surpasses the maximum EBS IOPS or throughput that the instance can support. These metrics return a value of 0 when performance is not exceeded and 1 when it is. Users can utilize these metrics to create customized dashboards and alarms, enabling automatic responses such as upgrading to larger instance sizes or different types with higher EBS-Optimized limits. These metrics are available at a 1-minute frequency and are free of charge for all Nitro-based Amazon EC2 instances with EBS volumes attached. They can be accessed via the EC2 console, CLI, or CloudWatch API in all Commercial AWS Regions, including AWS GovCloud (US) and China Regions. For more information, visit the EC2 CloudWatch Metrics documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/amazon-cloudwatch-metrics-monitor-ec2-instances-i-o-performance/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"What's New at AWS - Cloud Innovation & News","context":"Amazon EBS has introduced a new fault action in AWS Fault Injection Service (FIS) that enables users to inject I/O latency on their Amazon EBS volumes as part of controlled testing experiments. This action is designed to help users understand how their mission-critical applications respond to storage faults and fine-tune monitoring and recovery processes for high availability. EBS volumes are suitable for applications requiring low latency, such as Oracle, SAP HANA, and Microsoft SQL Server. The latency injection action simulates degraded I/O performance on the volume, triggering real-world signals like CloudWatch alarms and operating system timeouts during storage performance issues. Users can use pre-defined experiment templates or customize/create their own templates to meet specific testing needs. The experiments can be integrated into existing chaos engineering tests, continuous integration, and release testing, and multiple FIS actions can be combined in one experiment. The new action is available in all AWS Regions where FIS is available. To learn more, visit the EBS FIS actions user guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/09/aws-fis-action-inject-io-latency-on-ebs/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"troubleshooting-efs","skill_description":"Diagnoses and resolves Amazon EFS issues including mount failures, NFS timeouts, permission errors, throughput problems, and burst credit exhaustion. Use when the user has an EFS file system that is not mounting, returning errors, performing slowly, or showing access denied.\n\n\nServices: efs, ec2, iam, kms, vpc, cloudwatch\nTasks: debug, troubleshoot, diagnose\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: storage\n*","skill_name":"troubleshooting-efs"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"troubleshooting-s3-files","skill_description":"Diagnoses and resolves Amazon S3 Files issues including mount failures, permission errors, synchronization problems, and performance issues. Use when the user has an S3 file system that is not mounting, returning access denied, not syncing changes to S3, showing files in lost+found, or performing slower than expected.\n\n\nServices: s3, s3files, efs, iam, kms, cloudwatch\nTasks: debug, troubleshoot, diagnose\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: storage\n*","skill_name":"troubleshooting-s3-files"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify meaning and recommended remediation for EBS volume status io-performance:stalled event", "parent_id": "1cf090f1-c2d5-406b-b9ef-1a191142e437"}
